<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Volcae Arecomici</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 121 BCE (approx.) · capital Nemausus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Celtic people of eastern Languedoc whose sacred spring at Nemausus became the Roman city of Nîmes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Arecomici were the eastern branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a>, a Celtic people who settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herault-river">Hérault</a> in the 3rd century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> records that twenty-four villages were subject to their chief town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nemausus</a>, which grew up around a sacred spring.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in hilltop oppida such as Nages, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nemausus</a> had a stone tower, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tour-magne">Tour Magne</a>, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> arrived. They traded closely with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a> and struck coins with Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Their land came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 121 BCE and part of it was given to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nemausus</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony with rich monuments, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maison-carree">Maison Carrée</a> and the aqueduct carried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pont-du-gard">Pont du Gard</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/volcae-arecomici.jpg" alt="Volcae Arecomici" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Bronze,_fonds_celtique,_Ar%C3%A9comiques_-_btv1b11284540v_(2_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 121 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nemausus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arecomici</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-210&amp;focus=volcae-arecomici" aria-label="Volcae Arecomici on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4785 625 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Volcae Arecomici lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5105 881L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5105 881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5105 881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nemausus. <a href="/atlas?year=-210&amp;focus=volcae-arecomici">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

